WEBVTT

NOTE Episode: The One Quilla Kept

1
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:30.186
<v Old Timber>Mm. The music tells me it's story time. Come close by the pond, little ones — the spring's in full swing out here, the willow's heavy and green, and everything, just about everywhere you look, is busy as can be.</v>

2
00:00:30.536 --> 00:00:56.432
<v Old Timber>Here's the question I'd like you holding while I tell it. When everyone is rushing past the big, loud, important things — who looks after the small thing that nobody else even sees? And does that small thing matter, way down there in the mud where no one's looking? Hold onto that. The answer's in the story — and I believe God tucked it in there a long while before any of us thought to ask.</v>

3
00:00:57.132 --> 00:01:29.741
<v Old Timber>It happened on a bright spring morning, the kind where the whole Creek wakes up with a to-do list as long as your arm. Down at the dam, paws were patching and packing. Out on the meadow, the planting was on — seeds in, soil patted, the whole field going in before the warm spell turned. And right up the middle of it all ran the main path. You know the one. The path everybody uses, the path everybody hurries down, twenty times a day, eyes front, places to be.</v>

4
00:01:30.091 --> 00:01:59.095
<v Old Timber>Now — somebody was NOT hurrying that morning. Somebody small and gray and quiet had stopped stock-still in the middle of all that rushing, and was looking down at something in the mud, with her head tipped to one side. And over from the high rocks, soft as a leaf settling, came Quilla the chinchilla — the one who notices the things the rest of us go right past. She crouched down low, and she said,</v>

5
00:01:59.795 --> 00:02:16.887
<v Quilla>Well now. Look at you. Right in the middle of the busiest path in the whole Creek. One little flower, knocked flat in the mud, gray with footprints — and still trying. Still got one petal up, looking at the sun.</v>

6
00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:34.173
<v Old Timber>And before she could say another word, down the path came Bongo the bear cub, with his usual cheerful crash, a wobbling stack of planting-buckets piled up past his ears. He couldn't see over them, but that never stopped Bongo. He boomed,</v>

7
00:02:34.523 --> 00:02:52.086
<v Bongo>QUILLA! Big day! HUGE day! We're planting the WHOLE meadow before the warm spell turns! You can't stand still — there's no standing-still allowed today, it's a no-standing-still kind of morning, so come ON —</v>

8
00:02:52.436 --> 00:03:00.255
<v Quilla>Mind your big foot, little bear. There's a flower down here. And a bee on it.</v>

9
00:03:00.605 --> 00:03:19.944
<v Bongo>A — a flower? Where? ...Oh. THAT little squashed brown thing? Quilla, that's not a flower, that's a flower that already GAVE UP. It quit. Big day! Buckets! Bye!</v>

10
00:03:20.294 --> 00:03:30.595
<v Old Timber>And off he crashed — and crashed RIGHT into the planting-poles, which went down like a row of dominoes, every last one of them, all the way down the path.</v>

11
00:03:30.945 --> 00:03:41.716
<v Bongo>I MEANT to do that! That's — that's faster, actually! They're all down now, super easy to grab! You're WELCOME, everybody!</v>

12
00:03:42.066 --> 00:03:55.554
<v Old Timber>Quick behind him, going so fast she was three places at once, came Zippy the hummingbird, ticking off a list mid-air without landing once. She zipped straight through the falling poles and squeaked,</v>

13
00:03:55.904 --> 00:04:27.547
<v Zippy>Okay — okay. Spring-tidy status update: dam, sixty percent done. Meadow, forty percent and climbing. Path — clear and FAST, which is the WHOLE idea of a path, except — Quilla, you're a path-blockage. You're at zero percent moving. The path-flow-index is dropping, and YOU'RE the dropping, please drop less, this is a high-traffic morning —</v>

14
00:04:27.897 --> 00:04:33.626
<v Quilla>Your index can wait, little fast one. The bee can't. Watch where you buzz.</v>

15
00:04:33.976 --> 00:04:46.837
<v Old Timber>And high overhead, all bright feathers and big bold drama, sat Garingo the parrot. He flung out a wing so wide he nearly fell off the branch, and he called down,</v>

16
00:04:47.187 --> 00:05:07.205
<v Garingo>BEHOLD! THE GREAT SPRING BUSTLE! The day the Creek does the BIG things! The MIGHTY dam! The VAST meadow! The GLORIOUS tidy! The — wait. Quilla, what in the wide green world are you doing crouched down there in the MUD?</v>

17
00:05:07.555 --> 00:05:18.404
<v Quilla>Tending a small thing, theatrical one. The smallest thing on this whole loud morning. Somebody ought to keep an eye on it, and nobody else is going to.</v>

18
00:05:18.754 --> 00:05:40.627
<v Garingo>A SMALL thing? On the day of BIG things? Quilla, nobody will EVER know! There's no AUDIENCE down there! There's no crowd, there's no cheer, there's no — there's a bee. There's just one bee. ...A parrot needs a STAGE, Quilla. Onward — to GLORY!</v>

19
00:05:40.977 --> 00:05:59.663
<v Zippy>Okay — adding it to the list. Glory: pending. Stage: requested. Audience for Garingo: zero, right now, which is the very same as Quilla's flower, so really you two have the SAME numbers —</v>

20
00:06:00.013 --> 00:06:09.243
<v Garingo>We do NOT have the same numbers! ...Do we have the same numbers? ...Onward! Before the math catches me!</v>

21
00:06:09.593 --> 00:06:28.514
<v Old Timber>And last of all, slow and steady at the edge of it, came Shelly the tortoise — the one who watches everything and says almost nothing. She didn't hurry. She never does. She looked at the flower, and she looked at the whole rushing morning, and she said just this,</v>

22
00:06:28.864 --> 00:06:38.590
<v Shelly>...Everyone is running to plant a whole meadow. ...And nobody's watering the one flower that's already here.</v>

23
00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:43.311
<v Quilla>Somebody is, Shelly. ...Starting right now.</v>

24
00:06:43.661 --> 00:07:07.545
<v Old Timber>And up the rest of them went, into the big busy day, and the bustle swallowed them whole. And Quilla? Quilla stayed. She didn't make a speech about it. She didn't call anybody back to watch. She just looked at that one battered bloom, and that one tired bee, and she got quietly to work.</v>

25
00:07:08.245 --> 00:07:39.731
<v Old Timber>Now here's the part nobody saw — because nobody was looking. While the whole Creek banged and planted and hurried up and down that path, Quilla knelt in the mud beside it and did small things. No crowd. No cheering. No Garingo announcing it. Just her, and the flower, and the morning, and the work. And maybe — though she never said so — the quiet sense that God was right there in the small of it with her.</v>

26
00:07:40.431 --> 00:07:56.165
<v Old Timber>On the first morning, she built it a little fence. Three twigs, leaned together over the bloom, so the next big foot would step around instead of on. She pressed each one in slow, and tested it, and pressed it again.</v>

27
00:07:56.515 --> 00:08:09.036
<v Quilla>There. One. ...Two. ...Three. A little wall, so the busy world steps wide of you. Nobody trips a flower they can see.</v>

28
00:08:09.386 --> 00:08:35.595
<v Old Timber>On the second morning, the mud had gone dry and cracked and thirsty. So she carried water — in a curled leaf, all the way from the creek. And it spilled. Most of it, halfway across, soaked clean into the path. So she went back. And she curled the leaf tighter, and she carried it slower, holding her breath the whole way. And THIS time, it held.</v>

29
00:08:35.945 --> 00:08:54.082
<v Quilla>Hm. ...Slower, then. The little ones don't need a flood. They need a sip, and somebody who'll come back tomorrow and bring the next one. ...That's how God tends things too, I think. A little, faithfully, every day.</v>

30
00:08:54.432 --> 00:09:22.156
<v Old Timber>And on the third morning — and the fourth, and the fifth — she just came. Before the bustle woke up, before any foot hit the path, before the dam-tapping even started, Quilla was there. A twig nudged straight. A sip of water from the curled leaf. A look, to be sure the bee still had somewhere to land. That was all. That was the whole of it. Every single morning.</v>

31
00:09:22.506 --> 00:09:40.565
<v Old Timber>And then, on about the sixth morning, somebody finally noticed HER. At the edge of the clearing, soft-eyed and quiet, stood Fern the fawn, who'd been watching three days now and hadn't said a word. She came a step closer, and she whispered,</v>

32
00:09:40.915 --> 00:09:57.459
<v Fern>Um... Quilla? You come every single morning. Before everyone. And the flower's not even... pretty yet. It's still kind of gray. Why do you keep doing it, when nobody sees?</v>

33
00:09:57.809 --> 00:10:06.020
<v Quilla>Oh, little fawn. ...Come sit by me. Watch with me a minute, and I'll tell you the true answer, not the pretty one.</v>

34
00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:23.003
<v Quilla>I'll tell you the truth, Fern. ...Some mornings it does feel small. The dam gets cheered. The meadow gets a whole song. And me? I'm a gray chinchilla in the mud with one flower nobody's even named.</v>

35
00:10:23.353 --> 00:10:42.196
<v Quilla>There's a morning — there's always a morning — where you kneel down here in the cold and you think: why am I out here. Nobody would even know if I stopped. The flower wouldn't write me a thank-you note. The bee wouldn't notice for a week.</v>

36
00:10:42.546 --> 00:10:45.010
<v Fern>...So why don't you stop?</v>

37
00:10:45.360 --> 00:11:08.382
<v Quilla>Because it's mine to keep, little one. Somebody put a living thing right in the path where everybody hurries past it. ...And that morning, that somebody got to be me — deciding it mattered, when no one else did. And I think God sees it too — the small kept thing, the quiet keeping, even when there's not a single soul watching but Him.</v>

38
00:11:08.732 --> 00:11:23.108
<v Quilla>So you don't tend a small thing so somebody claps for you, Fern. You tend it because it's alive, and it's near you, and it's yours to keep. That's the whole of why. The keeping IS the reason.</v>

39
00:11:23.458 --> 00:11:40.080
<v Old Timber>And it was right then — soft as anything — that I'd wandered up the path myself, looking, the way I do, for the quiet part of a loud day. I didn't say much. I just sat down nearby, and I asked the one thing I'd been wondering all week.</v>

40
00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:49.686
<v Old Timber>Fern... you've watched her six mornings now. What do you think she's really been growing out here — the flower, or something else?</v>

41
00:11:50.036 --> 00:12:06.084
<v Fern>...Both, I think. She was growing the flower. ...And the flower was growing her. Into the kind of friend who keeps coming back. Who doesn't need anybody to see, to keep doing the good thing.</v>

42
00:12:06.434 --> 00:12:08.245
<v Old Timber>Mm.</v>

43
00:12:08.945 --> 00:12:38.524
<v Old Timber>Now — you'd think that's the end of it. Quiet chinchilla, quiet flower, nice and small, and off to bed. But spring had a turn left in it. Because a hard, late frost came creeping through one night — the kind that comes after everybody has stopped expecting it — and it bit the new meadow. Bit it cruel. By morning, nearly every fresh bloom the whole busy Creek had planted stood brown and bowed and bitten.</v>

44
00:12:38.874 --> 00:13:06.285
<v Old Timber>And a long, worried silence rolled down the path. All that planting. All that hurry. All those big things — and the cold had undone them in one night. The bees came out into the gray morning, and there was nothing open for them. Nothing at all. Nothing to drink, nowhere to land, no seed to carry. The whole bright spring just... stalled.</v>

45
00:13:06.635 --> 00:13:35.979
<v Old Timber>And down the main path the worried Creek came — Bongo, Zippy, Garingo, Shelly, the whole bustle, walking slow now, looking for one green thing the frost had missed. And there — fenced in three little leaning twigs, watered every morning by somebody who never once stopped — stood ONE bloom. Open. Bright. Wide awake. And alive with bees.</v>

46
00:13:36.679 --> 00:13:53.380
<v Bongo>It's... it's the squashed one. The one I said gave up. The one I said QUIT. Quilla — it's the only one left in the whole Creek. And the bees — look — the bees are taking it everywhere.</v>

47
00:13:53.730 --> 00:14:29.474
<v Zippy>Okay — okay. Re-checking the math. One bloom. Surviving. Times every single bee carrying its seed-dust out to the bare meadow, times every bare patch they touch down on, equals — oh. Quilla, your zero-percent flower is reseeding the ENTIRE field. By itself. That's not small. My index was wrong. The index was SO wrong. I am deleting the index.</v>

48
00:14:29.824 --> 00:14:51.645
<v Garingo>BEHOLD... the small thing. ...I declared the DAM. I declared the MEADOW. I declared the GLORIOUS tidy. And the whole spring was saved by one flower I flapped straight over without looking down. ...Friends. I have been very, very BIG about all the wrong sizes.</v>

49
00:14:51.995 --> 00:14:58.377
<v Shelly>...Funny. The biggest thing in the meadow today... is the smallest one.</v>

50
00:14:58.727 --> 00:15:04.064
<v Fern>She kept it when it was nothing. That's the whole reason it's something now.</v>

51
00:15:04.414 --> 00:15:33.340
<v Old Timber>And so it turned. While the rest of us were busy being big, our gray friend kept one small thing faithfully — morning after morning, with nobody watching at all — and the small kept thing is exactly what carried the whole spring through. You know, there's a line about this in the Bible. It's something Jesus told the people listening to him, written down in a book called Luke, chapter sixteen, verse ten. And it goes like this.</v>

52
00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:39.027
<v Old Timber>"He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in much."</v>

53
00:15:39.377 --> 00:15:51.114
<v Bongo>What's — what's that mean, Old Timber? Faithful in the least, faithful in the much? That's a lot of big old words.</v>

54
00:15:51.464 --> 00:16:29.585
<v Old Timber>Ah. It is, little bear. So here's the plain of it. "Faithful in that which is least" means: you can be trusted with the smallest thing of all — the one nobody else even bothers to see. And "faithful also in much" means that same quiet keeping, done over and over, is the very thing big trust is built out of. The small faithful thing and the big one are made of the same stuff, little bear. So God sees the small kept thing — the one nobody else even sees. And sometimes, when we're not even looking, He grows it into the very thing everybody needed.</v>

55
00:16:29.935 --> 00:16:56.954
<v Quilla>So here's a thing to carry home, little ones. There's a small good work near you right now that nobody's clapping for. A plant on a windowsill. A friend who's quiet today. A job that no one will ever thank you for. ...Tend it anyway. Keep it. Come back to it tomorrow. The keeping is the whole of it — and God sees every bit of it, even when no one else does.</v>

56
00:16:57.304 --> 00:17:01.910
<v Old Timber>That's how I remember it, anyway. Goodnight, little ones.</v>

57
00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:13.616
<v Old Timber>And from his perch on the lecture branch swooped Professor Hoot, the most certain owl in Shiloh Creek. He cleared his throat — ahem — and announced,</v>

58
00:17:13.966 --> 00:17:41.586
<v Professor Hoot>Children, gather round. Today, a fact known to be true beyond all doubt — and tied, you'll be glad to hear, to all that small, quiet tending our gray friend did this morning. Here it is, plainly. The nuthatch is the one small bird that climbs DOWN a tree trunk — headfirst, face to the ground, the wrong way round to every other bird in the wood.</v>

59
00:17:41.936 --> 00:18:15.866
<v Professor Hoot>Now — why should that matter to anyone? Because going DOWN headfirst, the little nuthatch sees the bark from an angle every OTHER bird misses. And so it spots dinner tucked away in a crevice. A crevice — that is merely a fancy word for a tiny crack — a tiny crack the up-climbers flapped straight past without a second glance. The small, easy-to-miss angle, you see, is precisely where the patient little nuthatch finds the supper everyone else walked right by.</v>

60
00:18:16.216 --> 00:18:27.823
<v Professor Hoot>It will even tuck a seed into that little crack and come back for it, days later, faithful as a clock. ...Pardon. As I was saying. Class dismissed.</v>

61
00:18:28.523 --> 00:18:41.775
<v Old Timber>And then, plip-plop down from the lily pads, came the Mudpuppy brothers — Splish, Splash, and Splosh — three frogs and one chorus of confusion. Splosh started,</v>

62
00:18:42.125 --> 00:18:49.213
<v Splosh>Today on Mudpuppy Minutes, we will explain... WHY do the STARS only come out at NIGHT!</v>

63
00:18:49.563 --> 00:19:03.860
<v Splish>Easy. The stars only come out at night because they are NOCTURNAL. Like owls. They sleep all day in a big star drawer, and at bedtime somebody slides the drawer OPEN. Out they pop.</v>

64
00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:26.187
<v Splash>No, no — the stars are ALWAYS up there. You just can't SEE them in the daytime, because the sun is so bright it washes them right out. Then the sun goes down, the sky goes dark, and — there they are! ...Wait. That sounded smart. Did I just do a smart?</v>

65
00:19:26.537 --> 00:19:48.436
<v Splosh>Oh no, no — Splash, that's far too EASY. Stars in the DAYTIME? Nonsense, I'd have tripped over one. Mudpuppies, mudpuppies — we have solved it. The stars come out at night because that's when the moon turns them ON. Big switch. Click. The end.</v>

66
00:19:48.786 --> 00:20:10.685
<v Old Timber>Now — just so you've got it straight, little ones: the stars are up there ALL the time, day and night. We only see them once the sky goes dark, because the sun's bright light hides them in the daytime. Splash had it for one shining second there. That's the real answer — but let's let the frogs keep their big moon switch.</v>

67
00:20:11.385 --> 00:20:15.521
<v Ranger Ray>See you next time, somewhere in Shiloh Creek.</v>
